 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls**
-*   **Global Sampling Failure:** Using global random sampling (as in Instant-NGP) to update the grid ignores previous occupancy values and transitions. In on-the-fly training with few iterations, this can miss occupied regions that have moved, resulting in "blank cubes" in rendered images [S1]. This method avoids that by updating probabilities at occupied points based on transition [S1].
-*   **Dynamic Scene Assumption:** The method is designed for dynamic scenes captured by multi-view forward-facing cameras. It may not be directly applicable to monocular or 360-degree inward-facing setups without modification, as these involve different motion characteristics and hardware constraints [S1].
